  "worked_solution": "To find the lowest common multiple of three numbers, we first find their prime factorizations. \n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $10.$ We easily deduce that $10=2\\cdot 5.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $24:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14167412/assets/cbf192e5a1a1b1ac9db41bb9c8427ff8cdebeb697bcb618da87689a628b8f3af.png)\n\nTherefore, $24={2}^{3}\\cdot 3.$\n\n- Then, we find the prime factorization of $18:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14167412/assets/9e7bddde8a287f6224b3e79f9fdacc3ac22d6db5a021b85df287e5e5fa321932.png)\n\nTherefore, $18=2\\cdot {3}^{2}.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =2\\cdot 5 \\\\ 24 & ={2}^{3}\\cdot 3 \\\\ 18 & =2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in any of the factorizations:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $3$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest  common multiple of $10$, $24$  and $18$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(10,24,18)={2}^{3}\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 5=360.\n$$",
